§ `VerifyManufacturedClausesFixture`

-- Positive control: rules with no redundant condition (smucclaw/l4-ide#1093).
--
-- The ladder's normal form is conjunctive, and distributing a disjunction of
-- conjunctions manufactures clauses nobody wrote. `(a AND b) OR ((NOT a) AND e)`
-- becomes `(a OR NOT a) AND (a OR e) AND (NOT a OR b) AND (b OR e)`, and the
-- last clause follows from the two before it. No condition in any rule below
-- is redundant -- every input changes the answer somewhere -- so `l4 verify`
-- must report nothing here and exit 0.
--
-- The parentheses around `NOT a` are load-bearing: on one line, NOT reaches to
-- the end of the line, so `NOT a AND e` reads as `NOT (a AND e)`.

-- The issue's rule, written as a disjunction.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, e IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `eligible` a b e IF
    (a AND b) OR ((NOT a) AND e)

-- The same rule, written as the choice it is.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, e IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `eligible, as a choice` a b e IF
    IF a THEN b ELSE e

-- Three limbs, none implied by the other two. Its normal form has twelve
-- clauses, several of them entailed by their siblings.
GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN, q IS A BOOLEAN, r IS A BOOLEAN, s IS A BOOLEAN, t IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `three ways in` p q r s t IF
       (p AND q)
    OR (p AND r AND s)
    OR (t AND s)

-- Rows that exclude one another. Each row also carries "no row above it
-- applied", and here the second row's own test already says so: `NOT a`
-- entails `NOT (a AND b)`. Nobody wrote that negation, so it is not reported.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exclusive rows` a b IF
  BRANCH IF a AND b THEN TRUE
         IF NOT a   THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE
